    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: Business Structure & Organization (S1-U2)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nUNIT OBJECTIVE: Students can explain what a business is in the sports/entertainment context and compare different types of business ownership.\nPLANNED WORK PRODUCT: Conglomerate structure document: formal description of the business structure — what type of organization it is, how divisions relate to each other, who has authority over what, and how the school store's required status fits into the overall design.\nCOURSE ROLE: Marketing students analyze the business structures of three real sports, entertainment, or esports organizations and present findings to the class. Focus on how structure affects marketing decisions.\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-68, 8175-69\n\nCRITERIA\n\nCriterion 1: Understanding the Business Structure of Sports, Entertainment, and Event Marketing\n- 8175-68 Explain business as it relates to the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    * Evidence of a definition of business\n    * Evidence of an understanding of business activities (e.g., management, accounting, finance, marketing, production, procurement)\n- 8175-69 Compare the types of business ownership and their roles in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    * Evidence of for-profit businesses\n    * Evidence of proprietorship\n    * Evidence of partnership\n    * Evidence of corporation\n    * Evidence of cooperative\n    * Evidence of franchise\n    * Evidence of nonprofit organizations\n    * Evidence of advantages and disadvantages of each type\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work passes when the planned work product shows evidence for every competency listed above, in the student's own words, applied to the class conglomerate (its divisions, school store, events and esports program) or a real organization in sports, entertainment or events, and uses the unit vocabulary accurately (Business ownership type, Organizational structure, Vertical integration, For-profit organization, Nonprofit organization, Division). Each criterion needs specific examples, not general statements. Work that restates definitions without applying them, or leaves any listed competency without evidence, does not pass.",
    "promptText": "Business Structure & Organization (S1-U2)\n\nStudents can explain what a business is in the sports/entertainment context and compare different types of business ownership.\n\nWhat you will make: Conglomerate structure document: formal description of the business structure — what type of organization it is, how divisions relate to each other, who has authority over what, and how the school store's required status fits into the overall design.\n\nYour role: Marketing students analyze the business structures of three real sports, entertainment, or esports organizations and present findings to the class. Focus on how structure affects marketing decisions.\n\nThe work: Students examine the conglomerate's structure formally: is it organized correctly? Does the reporting structure make sense? Are the divisions the right size? This is a management design exercise, not just a vocabulary lesson.\n\nGo deeper:\n\n- How business type affects marketing: a nonprofit youth league markets differently than a for-profit franchise. Students connect structure to marketing strategy decisions they'll make in later rotation tracks.\n- Task 68: the business landscape of sports, entertainment, and events — who are the major corporate players, and how are they structured?\n\nYour work must show evidence of each of these:\n\n- Explain business as it relates to the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n- Compare the types of business ownership and their roles in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n\nUse these terms accurately: Business ownership type, Organizational structure, Vertical integration, For-profit organization, Nonprofit organization, Division.\n\nWrite your response in the response zone below."
