    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: Business Structure & Organization (S1-U2)\nCOURSE: 8177 Sports Entertainment and Event Management\nUNIT OBJECTIVE: Students can explore organizational structures within the industry and explain for-profit vs. nonprofit operation.\nPLANNED WORK PRODUCT: Conglomerate structure document: formal description of the business structure — what type of organization it is, how divisions relate to each other, who has authority over what, and how the school store's required status fits into the overall design.\nCOURSE ROLE: Management students draft the formal org chart with reporting lines, decision authority, and division interdependencies. General Manager candidate writes the first draft of the management charter.\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8177-52, 8177-53\n\nCRITERIA\n\nCriterion 1: Explaining the Structure of Sports, Entertainment, and Event Businesses\n- 8177-52 Explore the organizational structures within the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    * Evidence of professional sports leagues\n    * Evidence of sports recreation leagues\n    * Evidence of general business studios\n    * Evidence of venues (public vs. private)\n    * Evidence of governing bodies (e.g., National Collegiate Athletic Association [NCAA], International Olympic Committee [IOC])\n    * Evidence of parks and recreation authorities\n    * Evidence of collegiate athletic programs\n- 8177-53 Explain the advantages and disadvantages of operating as a for-profit or nonprofit organization.\n    * Evidence of advantages of for-profit (e.g., business growth, investment potential)\n    * Evidence of disadvantages of for-profit (e.g., loss of revenue, business risk)\n    * Evidence of advantages of nonprofit (e.g., tax exemptions, more public support)\n    * Evidence of disadvantages of nonprofit (e.g., skepticism, insufficient volunteers, complex regulatory requirements)\n    * Evidence of evaluation of which alternative best aligns with the objectives\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work passes when the planned work product shows evidence for every competency listed above, in the student's own words, applied to the class conglomerate (its divisions, school store, events and esports program) or a real organization in sports, entertainment or events, and uses the unit vocabulary accurately (Business ownership type, Organizational structure, Vertical integration, For-profit organization, Nonprofit organization, Division). Each criterion needs specific examples, not general statements. Work that restates definitions without applying them, or leaves any listed competency without evidence, does not pass.",
    "promptText": "Business Structure & Organization (S1-U2)\n\nStudents can explore organizational structures within the industry and explain for-profit vs. nonprofit operation.\n\nWhat you will make: Conglomerate structure document: formal description of the business structure — what type of organization it is, how divisions relate to each other, who has authority over what, and how the school store's required status fits into the overall design.\n\nYour role: Management students draft the formal org chart with reporting lines, decision authority, and division interdependencies. General Manager candidate writes the first draft of the management charter.\n\nThe work: Students examine the conglomerate's structure formally: is it organized correctly? Does the reporting structure make sense? Are the divisions the right size? This is a management design exercise, not just a vocabulary lesson.\n\nGo deeper:\n\n- Organizational structures within the industry (Task 52): flat vs. hierarchical, functional vs. divisional. How does each affect a manager's day-to-day decisions?\n- For-profit vs. nonprofit tradeoffs (Task 53): if the school league became a nonprofit, what would change? Students evaluate this as a real strategic question.\n- Management specialization students map their own role within the org chart and write a one-paragraph role authority statement: what decisions can I make independently, and what requires approval?\n\nYour work must show evidence of each of these:\n\n- Explore the organizational structures within the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n- Explain the advantages and disadvantages of operating as a for-profit or nonprofit organization.\n\nUse these terms accurately: Business ownership type, Organizational structure, Vertical integration, For-profit organization, Nonprofit organization, Division.\n\nWrite your response in the response zone below."
