    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: Market Research (S3-U2)\nCOURSE: 8177 Sports Entertainment and Event Management\nUNIT OBJECTIVE: Students can explain market research applications, identify data sources, develop a market segmentation analysis, design a full research project, and evaluate results.\nPLANNED WORK PRODUCT: Three deliverables: (1) Market segmentation analysis — identifying and describing at least three distinct customer segments within the school community. (2) Full research project report — problem statement, methodology, data, analysis, findings, and recommendations. (3) Research presentation — 10-minute presentation to both classes, with Q&A, followed by a formal handoff document to the Wholesale Manager and League Manager.\nCOURSE ROLE: 8177 management students conduct the segmentation analysis (Task 81) and develop the full research project (Tasks 82–84) as the deeper assessment. Wholesale Manager uses findings to finalize the first purchase order. League Manager uses findings to set initial league registration targets and merchandise SKU priorities.\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8177-77, 8177-78, 8177-79, 8177-80, 8177-81, 8177-82, 8177-83, 8177-84\n\nCRITERIA\n\nCriterion 1: Understanding Market Research\n- 8177-77 Explain market research and its use in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    * Evidence of the purpose of market research (i.e., gathering information that will help guide marketing decisions to improve a company’s bottom line)\n    * Evidence of how market segmentation is used in market research\n    * Evidence of the process used to gather the information, analyze it, and report findings\n    * Evidence of application of research to all seven functions of marketing (i.e., how research drives promotion and product service management)\n- 8177-78 Explain the need for market research for all functions of marketing.\n    * Evidence of selling\n    * Evidence of marketing-information management\n    * Evidence of marketing planning\n    * Evidence of pricing\n    * Evidence of promotion\n    * Evidence of product-service management\n    * Evidence of distribution (channel management)\n- 8177-79 Identify sources of data for market research in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    * Evidence of surveys\n    * Evidence of test marketing\n    * Evidence of social media and blog observation\n    * Evidence of financial statements\n    * Evidence of point-of-sale data\n    * Evidence of consumer panels or focus groups\n    * Evidence of industry reports\n    * Evidence of academic articles\n    * Evidence of the role of AI in compiling and analyzing data\n- 8177-80 Describe external factors affecting the sports, entertainment, and event marketing process.\n    * Evidence of Describe external factors affecting the sports, entertainment, and event marketing process\n- 8177-81 Develop a market segmentation analysis.\n    * Evidence of identification of the type of product or business to be offered\n    * Evidence of an analysis of the target market in terms of types of segmentation\n- 8177-82 Describe the steps in developing a market research project.\n    * Evidence of Define the problem\n    * Evidence of Analyze the situation\n    * Evidence of Develop a process for collecting data\n    * Evidence of Collect, organize, and analyze the data\n    * Evidence of Determine a solution to the problem\n    * Evidence of Evaluate the solution\n    * Evidence of Prepare the report\n- 8177-83 Develop a market research project.\n    * Evidence of Develop a market research project\n- 8177-84 Evaluate the results of a market research project.\n    * Evidence of analyzing data\n    * Evidence of proposing a solution\n    * Evidence of reporting findings to the management team\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work passes when the planned work product shows evidence for every competency listed above, in the student's own words, applied to the class conglomerate (its divisions, school store, events and esports program) or a real organization in sports, entertainment or events, and uses the unit vocabulary accurately (Market research, Market segmentation, Primary research, Secondary research, Price sensitivity, Data triangulation, Recommendation). Each criterion needs specific examples, not general statements. Work that restates definitions without applying them, or leaves any listed competency without evidence, does not pass.",
    "promptText": "Market Research (S3-U2)\n\nStudents can explain market research applications, identify data sources, develop a market segmentation analysis, design a full research project, and evaluate results.\n\nWhat you will make: Three deliverables: (1) Market segmentation analysis — identifying and describing at least three distinct customer segments within the school community. (2) Full research project report — problem statement, methodology, data, analysis, findings, and recommendations. (3) Research presentation — 10-minute presentation to both classes, with Q&A, followed by a formal handoff document to the Wholesale Manager and League Manager.\n\nYour role: 8177 management students conduct the segmentation analysis (Task 81) and develop the full research project (Tasks 82–84) as the deeper assessment. Wholesale Manager uses findings to finalize the first purchase order. League Manager uses findings to set initial league registration targets and merchandise SKU priorities.\n\nThe work: Students conduct a full market research project on the school community as a consumer base. Segmentation analysis identifies which student groups are most likely to buy esports merchandise, attend events, or use the school store. Findings are presented to the full class and formally handed to the Wholesale Manager as sourcing guidance.\n\nGo deeper:\n\n- Market segmentation analysis (Task 81): management students produce a formal segmentation document with at least three segments, each described by demographic, psychographic, and behavioral characteristics, with a recommended marketing approach for each.\n- Full research project development (Tasks 82–83): the 8177 research project goes beyond the 8175 survey — it includes observational data (store traffic patterns, lunch period behavior) alongside survey data, and uses secondary sources to triangulate findings.\n- Results evaluation (Task 84): management students write a formal evaluation of the research project — what did it answer, what did it fail to answer, what would the next research cycle address, and what decisions should be made based on the evidence?\n\nYour work must show evidence of each of these:\n\n- Explain market research and its use in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n- Explain the need for market research for all functions of marketing.\n- Identify sources of data for market research in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n- Describe external factors affecting the sports, entertainment, and event marketing process.\n- Develop a market segmentation analysis.\n- Describe the steps in developing a market research project.\n- Develop a market research project.\n- Evaluate the results of a market research project.\n\nUse these terms accurately: Market research, Market segmentation, Primary research, Secondary research, Price sensitivity, Data triangulation, Recommendation.\n\nWrite your response in the response zone below."
